e('## Why real LRU is impossible','## Why exact page LRU is expensive','Notfundamentallyimpossible.')
e('True LRU needs to reorder a list on *every memory access*: billions per second. No OS can see accesses at that rate, and no CPU records timestamps per page. All the kernel has is the **A bit**, which the MMU sets on any access and only software clears.','Exact LRU needs enough information to order every page\'s latest use, whether maintained as a list or another structure. Commodity page tables do not expose a complete recency trace to the OS, and trapping every access is prohibitively expensive for ordinary workloads. Access bits and sampled/software observations support approximations.','Notonlylist/noCPUever broadfalse.',lr)
e('If every bit is 1, the hand clears them all and comes back to its start, which degrades to FIFO.','If every bit is 1 and no accesses intervene, the hand clears them and returns to its starting frame. This resembles FIFO for that scan; it does not make every subsequent clock decision identical to FIFO.','Concurrentbits/model scope.')
e('def clock_victim(frames, abit, hand):\n    while True:','def clock_victim(frames, abit, hand):\n    # Nonempty equal lengths, valid hand.\n    # No concurrent reference-bit updates.\n    while True:','Statehelperinput/concurrencycontract.')
e('- New file pages start on the **inactive** list. Only a second access promotes them to active. A one-off scan fills the inactive list and is evicted from there without ever disturbing the hot active pages: scan resistance.','- In the classic design, file-page admission to inactive lists and promotion based on observed reuse help resist one-pass scans. Refault handling and access paths add exceptions; active pages are not immune to pressure or scans.','Secondaccess/noteverdisturbfalse.',vm)
e('If it came back quickly, it is activated immediately, so a thrashing working set is recognised.','Refault distance and related history can influence activation. This is a workload-size/eviction-history signal, not simply elapsed wall-clock time.','Refaultdistancevswallclock.',lr)
e('Google reported lower reclaim CPU use and fewer low-memory kills on Android and ChromeOS.','Its documentation describes aging, eviction and refault feedback for workload-dependent reclaim decisions.','Uncitedbenchmarkclaimremoved.',lr)
e('- Below **min**: an allocating process must do **direct reclaim** itself, stalling inside `malloc` or a page fault.','- An allocation that cannot be satisfied may enter **direct reclaim** if its allocation flags allow it. Watermarks are part of that decision; memory-cgroup limits, fragmentation, allocation order and reclaim restrictions also matter. Not every allocation below min performs reclaim.','GFP/reclaimfalseuniversal.',vm)
e('If they do not fit, every fault evicts a page that someone needs soon,','When sustained demand outruns available resident capacity, repeated faults can evict pages needed again soon,','Notmathematicaleveryfault.')
e('Symptoms on Linux: the disk is saturated with swap I/O, `vmstat` shows high `si`/`so`, CPU time is mostly iowait, and an SSH login takes minutes.','Possible symptoms include high refault/reclaim rates, swap or file I/O, pressure stalls and poor responsiveness. Thrashing does not require swap, a saturated disk, a particular iowait value or a fixed login delay.','Filebacked/CPUreclaimcanthrash.',ps)
e('Adding CPU or running more processes makes it worse.','Adding CPU does not solve the illustrated capacity shortage; adding memory-demanding jobs can worsen it.','NotallmoreCPUmakesworse.')
e('kill workloads when pressure stays high, long before the kernel\'s own OOM killer would act.','can kill selected workloads according to configured pressure and cgroup policies before a kernel allocation failure; timing and enabled policies vary.','No universalearlier guaranteed.',ps+['https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd-oomd.service.html'])
e('- Exact LRU is impossible to implement for pages; clock (second chance) approximates it with the hardware accessed bit, and the dirty bit refines the choice.','- Exact page recency tracking is generally too costly on commodity systems; clock approximates it with access bits, and dirty state informs writeback cost.','Summaryavoidimpossible.')
e('- Thrashing happens when the sum of working sets exceeds RAM; the fix is less memory demand, not more CPU. PSI measures it directly.','- Excess resident demand can cause repeated eviction/refault and poor throughput. PSI measures memory stalls, which can have causes beyond thrashing.','PSInotdirectthrashproof.',ps)
