f(9,'explanation','Correct as a simplified description of scan-resistant admission. Exact promotion depends on observed references, refault history and the kernel; active pages can still be reclaimed under pressure.','Classicmodelqualification.',vm,o=1)
f(9,'workedExample','Inactive admission and reuse-based promotion reduce pollution compared with pure LRU, but do not guarantee that 40 GiB of hot data survives a 500 GiB scan. Reclaim scope, capacity and kernel policy matter. POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED can advise dropping already-cached file data; it does not bypass the cache in the way suitable O_DIRECT I/O can. Hints and direct-I/O alignment/support requirements must be checked.','FADVdoesnotbypasscaching.',fa)
f(10,'text','Reduce concurrent memory demand enough to leave RAM headroom, or add RAM; three jobs are a possible starting point to measure','15GiBnotguaranteedfit16incloverhead.',vm,o=3)
f(10,'explanation','Correct. Twenty GiB of active demand exceeds sixteen GiB before other consumers. Three nominal five-GiB jobs may still leave too little headroom, so measure pressure rather than assuming a fixed safe concurrency.','Capacityheadroom.')
f(10,'workedExample','Four five-GiB working sets total twenty GiB, exceeding sixteen GiB before kernel and other memory. The stipulated repeated paging explains low useful CPU work. More jobs increase demand. Reduce concurrency or working-set size, retain headroom, or add RAM. Three jobs might help, but neither fitting nor a two-hour completion time follows from the stated numbers.','Removeinventedthroughputprediction.')
